// F239's cost, pinned. Once a named call may omit an `Option<T>`, two declarations the checker
// calls distinguishable -- these differ in `b`'s type, and `pick(a: 1, b: "s")` picks one -- can
// still be reached by a call that leaves out the parameter they differ in. That call is
// genuinely undecided, so it is refused here rather than at the declarations: the set is fine,
// this one call is not, and the message says which argument would settle it.
fn pick(a: int, b: Option<string>) -> string {
    return "string"
}

fn pick(a: int, b: Option<int>) -> string {
    return "int"
}

fn main() {
    print(pick(a: 1))
}
